  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days...\n\nAn upper level low pressure system / trough axis remains centered\nover the Great Basin today. This is allowing for our region to get\nrepeated vort maxes as they migrate northeastwards. Several more\nweak waves of energy will move up from the Great Basin into\nMontana through the day Saturday. This will result in rounds of\nshowers and thunderstorms over the next 2-3 days with periods of\nrelatively dry conditions in the areas of AVA behind these\nshortwaves. \n\nNow that we are firmly under the shield of clouds there will be\nlittle severe threat with any of these storms due to the lack of\nCAPE and increased CIN. What remains a threat though the the\npotential for locally heavy rainfall. While no storm is forecast\nto have overly impressive rainfall rates, repeated showers over\nthe same areas could cause some localized flooding. That being \nsaid, we have been quite dry over the past month which means that \nthe ground should be able to hold most of this moisture just fine\nwith minimal flooding concerns on a region-wide scale. \n\nDue to the relative weak steering flow the upper level low will be\nallowed to naturally retrograde over the next several days before\nmerging with a secondary advancing low pressure system. This will\nput our area back under a ridge as it builds back in from the \nGreat Plains. The good news with this feature though is that it \nwill not be nearly as hot as the previous ridging pattern. Surface\nwinds will remain primarily out of the plains, limiting any \ndownslope effects and keeping the neer-surface environment \ndecently moist. Additionally, the apex of this ridge will be \nroughly along the MT/SK border meaning that we could see \nshortwaves ride along the ridge bringing chances for brief \ncooldowns and / or precipitation. One such trough could impact the\narea this time next week. WMR",
